    Well, I've posted here before about tips for polypiling.  But the
last time I posted was pl7, and it was still possible to find wands of
wishing and magic lamps in a polypile.  That`s gone now.  But
polypiling is still a great way to pick up your game.

    This post discusses the major goals in polypiling.  It also outlines
a neat form of polypile abuse that hasn't, to my knowledge, been
discussed on the net before.  At least, it doesn't appear in the Wheaton
spoiler, and it still works in pl9 (we don't have 10 installed here
yet.)  This involves a complicated dance using ice boxes, cockatrice
corpses and shops.  But first the easy stuff.

     The first thing to know is that, although you can't get wands of
wishing in the polypile, you CAN get new wands of polymorph -- a wand
of light, for instance, can polymorph into a wand of polymorph.  
     But with wish-abuse gone, the main role of polypiling is for
turning junk into hard-to-find items.  These are mainly --
   Armor -if you start as a Wizard with a wand of polymorph, you'll
probably want to start a polypile fairly early.  Gather up all the
uncursed armor you can find - buy all the cheap armor you can afford
from the shops and get your pet to steal the rest of it.  Gather up any
black caps and other minor armor.  Also throw in the pile any unwanted
rings, tools, potions, scrolls, spellbooks or low-quality food
(corpses, fortune cookies...).  Zap the pile once just to get some
good armor.

    Once you have reasonably good armor, take some time to assemble the
polypile.
    There are five main goals to polypiling - in roughly this order:
     (1) Getting good armor.
     (2) Enchanting that armor to a high degree.
     (3) Enchanting a weapon to a high degree.
     (4) Getting good rings.
     (5) Getting good spells.
  Goals 2 and 3 involve changing scrolls into scrolls of enchant armor
and enchant weapon.  Getting these scrolls is usually one of the main
bottlenecks in polypiling.  For this reason, I try to conserve scrolls
as much as possible - I try to avoid reading any scrolls until I can get
holy water to enchant them.  And even then, I try to avoid reading any
scroll until I've turned it into a blessed enchanting scroll.
   For this reason, getting holy water is an important secondary goal
for polypiling.  And, as I will discuss, it also makes it important to
put tools into the polypile.

   Getting holy water -- Of course the most convenient way to get holy
water is to drop water on a lawful alter, but I'm assuming that you
don't have one convenient.  If you have any potions that are blessed,
[and you don't yet have holy water] put them in a separate pile adjacent
to your pile.  Items keep their blessed/cursed state through
polymorphing, so if a blessed potion turns to water during a polymorph,
you've made holy water.  If you're polymorphing potions whose blessed
status is unknown to you, pick up water and look for a water [always a
clear potion] which doesn't combine with the other water in your
inventory.  When you have such a water, dip unblessed water into the
other water.  You will learn it's status that way, and you can tell
which status it has.
   If you're unsure which water is unblessed, you can always make a
neutral potion of water by dipping ANY potion [no matter what its
blessing stat] into a fountain twice.  Also, shopkeepers charge $5 for
plain water and won't buy it back, but charge $100 for holy water and
unholy water.
     This may be the place to mention the behavior of toxic potions vis a
vis unicorn horns.  If you #dip a unicorn horn into a potion of
sickness, hallucination or confusion, it will clear.  HOWEVER, it will
also clear it's blessed/cursed status, so you cannot make potions of
(un)holy water this way.
   If you have ANY alter handy, you can convert it to lawful if you are
a lawful character.  If you started out lawful, no problem.  Otherwise,
watch the polypile for odd helmets.  Try on any (uncursed) helmet,
looking for one that changes your alignment.  If you go from, say,
neutral to chaotic, keep taking it off and putting it on until it flips
you to lawful.  Then go kill things and sacrifice them until the alter
is lawful.

   If you feel safe on armor for the moment, go around and get all of
the tools from previous levels  (and maybe from the next couple levels.)
Yes, this includes dragging all those heavy boxes and chests down to the
polypile.  Also killing every dwarf and nymph you can, for their tools.
[Shopkeepers usually carry lock picks, but this is probably not worth
killing one for.]  It used to be that we polymorphed tools just to get
magic lamps.  But now we're looking for:
      magic markers
      ice boxes
      crystal balls
  Stethescopes, lamps, lock picks, leashes and cameras are all also
rather useful, and you may want to fish them out.
   Magic markers are an important way of turning mundane scrolls into
enchanting scrolls.  Erase the scroll, (by dipping in potions of plain
water, or in fountains, or in pools of water, or using a wand of
cancellation.)  NOTE:  Blessed magic markers write blessed scrolls on
uncursed blank scrolls.  Magic markers can be recharged to (50) using a
blessed scroll of charging, but if you have a large number of tools in
the polypile you should probably just throw the marker back on the pile
when it gets low, because another (fully charged one) will show up.
Blessed scrolls of charging are important for recharging your wand of
polymorph, however.
   Crystal balls are important for finding the scrolls of teleportation
that are hidden in the walls around the dungeon.  When you first get a
crystal ball, go back up through previous levels, looking for "?"s until
the ball is exhausted.  (You'll probably need a pick-axe to get to the
scrolls.)  Then go down through levels below you, again looking for more
scrolls and bringing what you find back to the polypile.
   Try and identify the things you find (and the things that you make in
the polypile) as much as possible without using up scrolls of identify.
Here's one of the ways that having MUNDO amounts of armor and rocks
(Yes, rocks) in the polypile can help you.  Cheap armor gets polymorphed
into plate mail, rocks get polymorphed into gems.  Sell this armor and
these gems back to the storekeeper for gold.  Then you can affford to
sell unidentified things to the shopkeeper and buy them back, paying his
markup.  So, for instance, LIGHT is the only $50 scroll in the game.
Shopkeepers pay half value for things.  So if you sell a scroll to the
shopkeeper for $25, you can #name that scroll LIGHT, and not have to use
up an identify on it.  
     Likewise, there are 3 scrolls worth $200 - taming, amnesia and
create monster.  None of these is worth using up a scroll for.  So if
you sell a scroll called ELBIB YLOH for $100, you can #name that kind of
scroll ee200, and you'll know what is is well enough to know that its
not worth identifying further.  Similar analysis hold for partially
identifying rings, spellbooks, wands and potions.  [See the Wheaton
spoiler file for prices.] 
     RINGS - Once you know that they're not cursed, several rings can be
ID'ed just by trying them on.  There is a small danger in this - if you
try on a ring of polymorph, there is a slight chance that you'll
polymorph before you can take it off.  But this chance is small (and is
only a problem for rings costing $300.)   
   Although rings can be identified by tossing them into sinks, I do not
recommend this, as it cost you the ring.  (The messages are very cute
however, and the dev team gets a couple of vigorous nods for this.)
    The strategy for polypiling rings is a little different than for
armor.  Most armor is not cursed, armor is plentiful, most cursed armor
is negatively enchanted, and the negative enchantment is carried over
through polymorphing.  Therefore, we just leave cursed armor out of the
polypile.  [with an exception for armor from ghost piles, see below.]
    Rings are harder to find, many of them are cursed, and the
enchantment does NOT carry over across the polymorph (for instance, a -2
ring of strength can turn into a +1 ring of adornment.)  Therefore, we
remove curses from cursed rings and toss them into the pile.
    Curses can be removed from rings (and other cursed items) using holy
water, scrolls of remove curse, wands of cancellation, or prayer.
Normally, I don't like to use up a scroll on curse removal insamuch as
scrolls are in shorter supply than potions; I prefer holy water.  I will
make an exception for ghost hoards - these often contain cursed but
positively enchanted armor.   In such cases, take a BLESSED scroll of
remove curse and gather up as much cursed stuff as you can find and
carry - this may mean taking off and dropping your regular armor and
putting on the armor you found in the bones pile.  Since a blessed
scroll of remove curse uncurses everything in your pack, the loss of one
scroll make more sense than the use of many potions of holy water.
Also, a wand of cancellation would strip the positive enchantments off
the armor, which is undesirable.  NOTE: don't bother to uncurse wands,
it doesn't make any difference if they are cursed.
   Particularly with rings, prayer may be a good way to remove curses.
If you've slaughtered a squad of soldiers, you may have a large stack of
cursed rings of protection.  If you don't have a wand of cancellation
handy, and your armor is pretty good, and you're on good terms with your
gods, you may just want to put these cursed rings on and pray at an
alter.  They'll usually become uncursed; if not, you can still use holy
water on them.  And prayer is a renewable resource in a way that holy
water, scrolls and wand charges are not.  When uncursed, throw 'em on
the polypile.
    Another minor goal of polypiling is getting a stack of
amulets of life-saving.  Unforunately, all amulets cost the same, so
you'll have to ID em.  Also, because of the behavior of the amulet of
change, even when you've uncursed them it's not safe to put them on to
test them.  But I've had as many as six such amulets.  This made me
feel very good, but I died on the pl9 bug having to do with choking
and amulets anyway (see the Wheaton file for details.)   Alas.
    Still another minor goal of polypiling is gluttony.  Once you've
got teleport and teleport control, it's real easy to burn off calories
very, very fast, so maybe you want lots of food rations.  It's worth
fishing a tinning kit out of the polypile for a while for this.  Then
when you clean out, say, a treasure zoo or a beehive, you can make
bunches of tins, which can all be polyed into better food.  Also, if
you're polypiling early in the game, it may be worth waiting until you
have a supply of 2 or 3 corpses you can throw on the polypile, just to
tide you over until the monsters get big enough to make a meal on.

    KEYSTONE COP ABUSE - DIALING FOR SPELLBOOKS.  
    [You need to have at least -6 armor for this, and you must have
gloves.  Speed, teleport, teleport control and invisibility are also
very helpful.]
    Spellbooks can be found inside of statues, at a frequency low enough
that some players do not believe that this actually ever happens.
Nonetheless.  Ahem.
    Statues can be created by bopping creatures with a cockatrice
corpse.  However, statues so created do not contain spellbooks.  And
cockatrice corpses, being corpses, do not last indefinitely.
    However, get yourself an ice box and kill a cockatrice.  (I aviod
killing cockatrices, if I can, until I get an icebox, just so I can
play this.)  Put the cockatrice cropse in the ice box so it won't
disappear.  Leave the box in or near a shop.  If you have teleport and
teleport control, fine.  Otherwise, dig a hole in the wall so you can
rob the shop easily.
   Now rob the shop of something cheap.  Take the cockatrice out of the
box and wield it.  Make MANY statues of Keystone Kops, but don't stone
the shopkeeper.  When you've stoned all the nearby Kops, pay off the
shopkeeper.  Repeat until you get very very bored or until the corpse
expires.  Put the corpse back in the box if you still have it, or
polymorph yourself into a Xorn and eat it, if you feel like showing off.
    Minor hint - you'll probably want to do all this fighting
blindfolded so that you can see how to teleport in and out of the shop
and so that you don't keep getting messages about cream pies in your
face.
    Assemble the statues into a polypile (this is why I told you to
stone all of the NEARBY kops.  Dragging Kop statues across the dungeon
is boring, even by polypile standards.)  Also throw all those damn cream
pies on the pile.  Polymorph the pile once.  Then smash ALL the statues
in a single blow, using a wand of striking or a spell of force bolt.
Your yield from one cockatrice corpse should be on the order of 15
spellbooks.  (this may depend on your luck, I don't know.)  Now if
you've created your polypile in the shop, the shopkeeper thinks he owns
these spellbooks.  This is fine, because he'll tell you how much money
he wants for them, and that helps you ID them.
   Once I get big stacks of spellbooks, I aim at polymorphing them into
good ones.  [Really, what good is a spellbook of detect food?  If you're
hungry, you don't have enough strength for the spell.  If you're not
hungry, you'll find the food soon enough from exploring.]  I
particularly like to have LOTS of spellbooks of magic mapping, as Hell
is VERY tedious without maps.
   VARIATION ON THIS METHOD -- large boulders can also be polymorphed
into statues.  It may amuse you to polymorph yourself into a giant and
go around picking up all the boulders in the dungeon and tossing them
into the polypile.  Giants can wear boots, helms, gloves and shields, so
this is pretty safe to do.  Curiously, there seems to be NO LIMIT on the
number of boulders a giant can carry - up to the point where your pack
gets full.   You need the appropriate rings for this.  On the other
hand, you don't need a cockatrice corpse or an ice box.


